var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","title":"API reference","text":"The package defines a single module OracleD. Docstrings are spliced in below; every entry corresponds to a public binding of the module. The module does not export anything, so these names are accessed as OracleD.<name>.","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Entry-point","page":"API reference","title":"Entry point","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Simulation-and-main-loop","page":"API reference","title":"Simulation and main loop","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Time","page":"API reference","title":"Time","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Jobs","page":"API reference","title":"Jobs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#VO-job-factories","page":"API reference","title":"VO job factories","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Cluster-and-worker-nodes","page":"API reference","title":"Cluster and worker nodes","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Cluster-loading","page":"API reference","title":"Cluster loading","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Job-scheduler","page":"API reference","title":"Job scheduler","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Data-logger","page":"API reference","title":"Data logger","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Logging","page":"API reference","title":"Logging","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Utilities","page":"API reference","title":"Utilities","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.main","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.main","text":"main() -> Simulation\n\nEntry point of the baseline simulation, the counterpart of the Python src/Main.py script: read config.json from the repository root, load the cluster inventory, create the run directory and logger, then build and run the Simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.Simulation","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.Simulation","text":"Simulation\n\nOwns the full simulation state: the clock, the carbon intensity data, the cluster, the job scheduler and the data logger. Constructing a Simulation performs all setup (including seeding the initial jobs) and prints the setup banner; start! then runs the main loop to completion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.start!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.start!","text":"start!(simulation)\n\nRun the simulation main loop until either all jobs have completed or the configured maximum simulated duration has passed, then print the summary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD._load_carbon_intensity_data","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD._load_carbon_intensity_data","text":"_load_carbon_intensity_data(path, datastart_str, datafinal_str)\n    -> Vector{CarbonIntensityEntry}\n\nRead the carbon intensity CSV, keeping the rows from the one whose datetime matches datastart_str up to (but excluding) the one matching datafinal_str. Rows with missing forecast or actual values produce the same console warnings as the Python code (and are stored as NaN).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.SimulationTime","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.SimulationTime","text":"SimulationTime\n\nThe shared simulated clock.\n\nFields:\n\ntime: the current simulated DateTime.\nstart_time: the simulated DateTime the run started from.\ntimestep_seconds: how far advance! moves the clock each step.\norigin: the real (wall-clock) DateTime the simulation was created at.\nverbosity: the configured output verbosity, used to gate log messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_to_current_time!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_to_current_time!","text":"set_to_current_time!(simulation_time)\n\nSet the simulated clock to the current wall-clock time, truncated to the minute (as the Python code does by round-tripping through a string).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_to_time!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_to_time!","text":"set_to_time!(simulation_time, time::DateTime)\n\nSet the simulated clock to time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.find_hh_segment","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.find_hh_segment","text":"find_hh_segment(simulation_time, dt, instruction = \"current\") -> DateTime\n\nFind the half-hour segment boundary associated with dt. With instruction == \"current\" this is the boundary at or before dt; with instruction == \"next\" it is the boundary strictly after dt (a dt exactly on a boundary counts as belonging to the previous segment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.advance!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.advance!","text":"advance!(simulation_time)\n\nMove the simulated clock forward by one timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_start_datetime","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_start_datetime","text":"get_start_datetime(simulation_time) -> DateTime\n\nGet the simulated start time of the run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_origin_datetime","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_origin_datetime","text":"get_origin_datetime(simulation_time) -> DateTime\n\nGet the real (wall-clock) time the simulation was started at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_current_datetime","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_current_datetime","text":"get_current_datetime(simulation_time) -> DateTime\n\nGet the current simulated time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_timestep","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_timestep","text":"get_timestep(simulation_time) -> Int\n\nGet the simulation timestep in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.Job","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.Job","text":"Job\n\nA single unit of work submitted to the cluster.\n\nFields:\n\nname: unique job name, e.g. \"ATLAS-Prod-17\".\nduration: the job length in seconds (scaled by node performance when the job starts).\nmemory_req: requested memory in GB per core.\ncores_req: number of cores (threads) requested.\nstart_time / end_time: simulated schedule of the job, UNSET_TIME until the job has been started. Use set_duration!, set_start_time! and set_end_time! to keep them consistent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_duration!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_duration!","text":"set_duration!(job, value)\n\nSet the job duration in seconds. If the job has already started, its end time is recomputed from the start time and the new duration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_start_time!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_start_time!","text":"set_start_time!(job, value::DateTime)\n\nSet the job start time; the end time is derived from the current duration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_end_time!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_end_time!","text":"set_end_time!(job, value::DateTime)\n\nDirectly set the job end time, for processes that change the life of a job while it is in progress (e.g. clocking a node up or down).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.AbstractVOJobFactory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.AbstractVOJobFactory","text":"AbstractVOJobFactory\n\nCommon supertype of the per-VO job factories. Each concrete factory stores the same state (tag, running job number, memory per core and the cached core request) and specialises get_duration and require_cores!.\n\nnote: Note\nAs in the Python original, the number of cores drawn for the first job is cached in cores_requested and reused for every subsequent job from the same factory. This quirk is preserved deliberately so the Julia simulation reproduces the Python results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.VOJobFactory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.VOJobFactory","text":"VOJobFactory(tag, RAM_per_core = 1, cores_req = 0) -> VOJobFactory\n\nBasic VO job factory producing short \"empty pilot\" jobs of ~15 minutes, with a 50% chance that all its jobs require 8 cores rather than 1. A cores_req of 0 means \"not yet decided\" (Python None).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.GridPPJobFactory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.GridPPJobFactory","text":"GridPPJobFactory(tag, RAM_per_core = 2, cores_req = 0) -> GridPPJobFactory\n\nTest VO factory that fixes all job variables: every job runs for exactly 5 hours on a single core (unless cores_req is given).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.ATLASJobFactory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.ATLASJobFactory","text":"ATLASJobFactory(tag, RAM_per_core = 2, cores_req = 0) -> ATLASJobFactory\n\nATLAS production job factory: ~42% short pilot jobs (~15 min), otherwise ~5 hour jobs; ~80% chance the factory produces 8-core jobs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.LHCbJobFactory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.LHCbJobFactory","text":"LHCbJobFactory(tag, RAM_per_core = 4, cores_req = 0) -> LHCbJobFactory\n\nLHCb production job factory: ~22% short pilot jobs, otherwise job lengths drawn from a Landau-like distribution between 9.5 and 18.5 hours; ~10% chance the factory produces 8-core jobs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_duration","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_duration","text":"get_duration(factory) -> Float64\n\nSample a job duration in seconds for a job from factory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.require_cores!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.require_cores!","text":"require_cores!(factory) -> Int\n\nReturn the number of cores jobs from this factory request. On the first call (when cores_requested == 0) the value is drawn randomly according to the VO's multi-core probability and cached for all later jobs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.debug_label","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.debug_label","text":"debug_label(factory) -> String\n\nThe debug-log message emitted when factory creates a job.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.create_job!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.create_job!","text":"create_job!(factory) -> Job\n\nCreate the next Job from factory, advancing its job counter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.WorkerNodeSpec","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.WorkerNodeSpec","text":"WorkerNodeSpec\n\nImmutable description of one machine type in the cluster inventory, the Julia counterpart of the dynamically generated Python WorkerNode subclasses.\n\nFields:\n\nnode_key: name of the machine type, e.g. \"DESYT3_0\"; used as the hostname prefix of instantiated nodes.\nthreads: number of (hyper)threads per machine.\nmemory_gb: RAM per machine in GB.\nidle_power_w: average instantaneous power dissipated by an idle node in W.\nfrequencies: available CPU frequencies in GHz, sorted descending (the first entry is the default maximum).\npowers_w: average instantaneous power dissipated in W by a fully occupied node at each frequency.\nhepscores: HEPScore23 benchmark score at each frequency.\nsystem, cpu_model, install_year: descriptive metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.WorkerNode","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.WorkerNode","text":"WorkerNode\n\nA simulated worker node.\n\nPower-related quantities are stored as energy use per second (the W values from the specification divided by 3600, as in the Python code). Job durations are rescaled when jobs start according to the node's HEPScore relative to the reference machine (see fixed_duration_multiplier), and again when the node is clocked up or down (dynamic_duration_multiplier).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.number_of_cores","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.number_of_cores","text":"number_of_cores(worker_node) -> Int\n\nNumber of job slots on the node. As in the Python original this is the number of threads (people interchange cores with threads when counting job slots); the physical-core count used for power scaling is the physical_cores field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.is_awaiting_jobs","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.is_awaiting_jobs","text":"is_awaiting_jobs(worker_node) -> Bool\n\nWhether the node has at least one free core.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_free_core_count","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_free_core_count","text":"get_free_core_count(worker_node) -> Int\n\nNumber of currently unoccupied cores (threads) on the node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_memory_available","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_memory_available","text":"get_memory_available(worker_node) -> Float64\n\nAmount of currently unallocated RAM on the node in GB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.can_schedule_job","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.can_schedule_job","text":"can_schedule_job(worker_node, job) -> Bool\n\nWhether job fits on the node right now. Note that, as in the Python original, only the per-core memory request is checked against the free memory (not memory_req * cores_req).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.set_running_frequency!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.set_running_frequency!","text":"set_running_frequency!(worker_node, value)\n\nSet the node's running frequency, recording the previous one. Throws an error if value is not one of the node's available frequencies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.start_job!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.start_job!","text":"start_job!(worker_node, job)\n\nStart job on the node: stamp its start time, rescale its sampled duration by the node's performance (fixed multiplier and current-frequency HEPScore) and allocate cores and memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.timestep_power_dissipated","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.timestep_power_dissipated","text":"timestep_power_dissipated(worker_node) -> Float64\n\nThe amount of energy used by the machine in one timestep, in kWh. Machines always draw at least the idle power; above that, power scales linearly with the number of busy physical cores up to the fully-active power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.change_clock_speed!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.change_clock_speed!","text":"change_clock_speed!(worker_node, clockspeed)\n\nChange the clock frequency of the node to clockspeed (GHz) and rescale the remaining time of every running job by the current dynamic duration multiplier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.clock_down!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.clock_down!","text":"clock_down!(worker_node)\n\nDecrease the frequency of the node by one available frequency step, adjusting its active power draw and stretching running jobs by the ratio of HEPScores. Prints a message if the node is already at its lowest frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.clock_up!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.clock_up!","text":"clock_up!(worker_node)\n\nIncrease the frequency of the node by one available frequency step, adjusting its active power draw and shrinking running jobs by the ratio of HEPScores. Prints a message if the node is already at its maximum frequency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.update!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.update!","text":"update!(worker_node)\n\nAdvance the node by one timestep: finish any jobs whose end time has been reached, freeing their cores and memory and reporting them to the data logger.\n\nThe recorded duration of a finished job reproduces Python's timedelta.seconds, i.e. the whole-seconds part of the elapsed time with full days discarded.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate!(cluster)\n\nAdvance the cluster by one timestep: update all nodes, try to schedule queued jobs, apply the configured energy-saving policy, and report the timestep's energy, carbon and occupancy to the data logger. Sets mission_accomplished when no jobs are running or queued.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate!(scheduler)\n\nSubmit any regular incoming job batches whose cycle boundary coincides with the current simulated time. Nothing is submitted while the cluster is completely idle with an empty queue (the run is about to end).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.CarbonIntensityEntry","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.CarbonIntensityEntry","text":"CarbonIntensityEntry\n\nOne record of grid carbon intensity data: the start of a data segment, the forecast intensity and the actual intensity (both in gCO2e/kWh).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.Cluster","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.Cluster","text":"Cluster\n\nThe cluster to be simulated: a set of worker nodes, the queue of jobs waiting to be scheduled, and the state needed to apply the configured energy-saving policy against the grid carbon intensity data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_number_of_nodes","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_number_of_nodes","text":"get_number_of_nodes(cluster) -> Int\n\nTotal number of worker nodes in the cluster.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_number_of_cores","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_number_of_cores","text":"get_number_of_cores(cluster) -> Int\n\nTotal number of job slots (threads) across all worker nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.submit_job!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.submit_job!","text":"submit_job!(cluster, job)\n\nAdd job to the cluster's queue and notify the data logger.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.has_queued_jobs","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.has_queued_jobs","text":"has_queued_jobs(cluster) -> Bool\n\nWhether any jobs are waiting in the queue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.has_running_jobs","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.has_running_jobs","text":"has_running_jobs(cluster) -> Bool\n\nWhether any worker node is currently running jobs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.cluster_occupancy","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.cluster_occupancy","text":"cluster_occupancy(cluster) -> Float64\n\nThe fraction of all job slots that are currently busy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.load_frequency_map","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.load_frequency_map","text":"load_frequency_map(frequency_csv, strict = true)\n    -> Dict{String,Dict{Float64,Tuple{Float64,Float64}}}\n\nLoad the per-node-type frequency dependence map from CSV. Each entry maps a node key (type_subtype) to a dictionary of frequency GHz => (power W, HEPScore).\n\nWith strict = true malformed entries raise an error; otherwise they are logged and skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.load_cluster_inventory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.load_cluster_inventory","text":"load_cluster_inventory(machinegroups_inventory_csv, frequency_dependence_csv;\n                       cluster_name = nothing, strict = true)\n    -> Vector{Tuple{WorkerNodeSpec,Int}}\n\nLoad the cluster inventory as an ordered list of (machine type specification, quantity) pairs — the Julia counterpart of the Python {WorkerNodeSubclass: quantity} mapping, preserving the CSV row order.\n\nArguments\n\nmachinegroups_inventory_csv: path to the machine inventory CSV.\nfrequency_dependence_csv: path to the frequency dependence CSV.\ncluster_name: optional filter on the cluster(main_Puppet_hostgroup) column.\nstrict: if true, raise on malformed or missing data; if false, log a warning and skip the offending row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.JobScheduler","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.JobScheduler","text":"JobScheduler\n\nSeeds the cluster with an initial mix of jobs at construction and, if configured, submits further job batches at regular simulated intervals.\n\ninitial_job_mix maps VO names (\"ATLAS\", \"LHCb\", \"GridPP\", anything else is a basic VO job) to job counts. regular_incoming_jobs is a list of (job mix, cycle length in seconds) pairs; an empty list means no regular submissions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.DataLogger","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.DataLogger","text":"DataLogger\n\nAccumulates run statistics (job counts, CPU time, energy, carbon and occupancy) and produces the end-of-run summary on the console and in the run directory (summary.txt and summary.json).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.job_start!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.job_start!","text":"job_start!(datalogger, job, worker_node)\n\nRecord that job has started on worker_node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.job_finish!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.job_finish!","text":"job_finish!(datalogger, job, worker_node)\n\nRecord that job has finished on worker_node, accumulating its wallclock and CPU time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.energy_and_carbon_consumed!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.energy_and_carbon_consumed!","text":"energy_and_carbon_consumed!(datalogger, timestep_energy_consumed, timestep_carbon_consumed_per_unit_energy)\n\nAccumulate the energy dissipated in a timestep (kWh) and the corresponding carbon (energy times the grid carbon intensity in g/kWh).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.peaktime_energy_and_carbon_consumed!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.peaktime_energy_and_carbon_consumed!","text":"peaktime_energy_and_carbon_consumed!(datalogger, timestep_energy_consumed, timestep_carbon_consumed_per_unit_energy)\n\nAs energy_and_carbon_consumed! but accumulated into the peak-time (5pm-9pm) totals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.sum_occupancy!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.sum_occupancy!","text":"sum_occupancy!(datalogger, timestep_occupancy)\n\nAccumulate the cluster occupancy fraction for one timestep (divided by the number of timesteps when the simulation ends to give the average).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.print_summary","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.print_summary","text":"print_summary(datalogger, summary_file, additional_description,\n              total_simulated_time, timestepinsec, total_real_time)\n\nCompute the derived averages, print the run summary to the console and, when summary_file is true, append it to summary.txt and write the machine-readable summary.json in the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.SimLogger","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.SimLogger","text":"SimLogger\n\nA minimal file logger for the simulation, playing the role of the Python logging logger named CLUSTERSIM.\n\nUntil configure_logger! is called the logger is unconfigured and all messages are dropped (just as the Python logger has no handler before configure_logger() runs). Afterwards messages are appended to simulation.log in the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.get_logger","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.get_logger","text":"get_logger() -> SimLogger\n\nReturn the shared simulation logger, mirroring the Python Logging.get_logger() helper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.configure_logger!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.configure_logger!","text":"configure_logger!(logger::SimLogger, debug::Bool, run_dir::AbstractString)\n\nPoint logger at <run_dir>/simulation.log and set whether debug messages are recorded. Any previously opened log file is closed first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.close_logger!","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.close_logger!","text":"close_logger!(logger::SimLogger)\n\nClose the log file (if open) and return the logger to its unconfigured state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.log_info","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.log_info","text":"log_info(logger::SimLogger, message)\n\nRecord an INFO-level message. Dropped if the logger is not yet configured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.log_warning","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.log_warning","text":"log_warning(logger::SimLogger, message)\n\nRecord a WARNING-level message. Dropped if the logger is not yet configured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.log_debug","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.log_debug","text":"log_debug(logger::SimLogger, message)\n\nRecord a DEBUG-level message. Only written when the logger was configured with debug = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.normalize_verbosity","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.normalize_verbosity","text":"normalize_verbosity(raw_verbosity, default = \"high\") -> String\n\nMap the configured verbosity onto one of (\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"), returning default when the value is missing or invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.default_run_label","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.default_run_label","text":"default_run_label(config) -> String\n\nBuild the fallback run label <total initial jobs>jobs_<savings policy> used when output.run_label is not present in the configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.slugify","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.slugify","text":"slugify(value) -> String\n\nReduce value to a lower-case filesystem-friendly slug: runs of non-alphanumeric characters become _, leading/trailing _ are stripped, and an empty result becomes \"simulation\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.create_run_directory","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.create_run_directory","text":"create_run_directory(config) -> String\n\nCreate a unique per-run output directory named <timestamp>_<run label slug> below output.log_dir (default logs/runs), store it in config[\"output\"][\"run_dir\"] and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.project_root","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.project_root","text":"project_root() -> String\n\nReturn the absolute path of the ORACLE-D repository root, i.e. the parent directory of the OracleD.jl package directory.\n\nAll relative paths found in config.json (data files, log directories) are resolved against this directory, so the simulation behaves the same regardless of the current working directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.resolve_path","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.resolve_path","text":"resolve_path(path) -> String\n\nResolve path against project_root unless it is already absolute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.weighted_choice","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.weighted_choice","text":"weighted_choice(values, probabilities) -> eltype(values)\n\nDraw a single random element from values with the given (normalised) probabilities. This is the equivalent of the Python numpy.random.choice(values, p=probabilities) call used to model the LHCb job-length distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.gauss","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.gauss","text":"gauss(mu, sigma) -> Float64\n\nSample from a normal distribution with mean mu and standard deviation sigma, mirroring Python's random.gauss(mu, sigma).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.pystr","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.pystr","text":"pystr(dt::DateTime) -> String\n\nFormat a DateTime the way Python's str(datetime) does, e.g. \"2024-01-16 16:00:00\". Milliseconds are included only when non-zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.pynum","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.pynum","text":"pynum(x) -> String\n\nFormat a number the way Python's string interpolation would: integers without a decimal point, everything else via string. Used where the Python code prints values taken straight from config.json.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#OracleD.total_seconds","page":"API reference","title":"OracleD.total_seconds","text":"total_seconds(period) -> Float64\n\nReturn the total number of seconds in a Dates period difference between two DateTimes, mirroring Python's timedelta.total_seconds().\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"custom-cluster/#Custom-cluster-makeup","page":"Custom cluster makeup","title":"Custom cluster makeup","text":"While ORACLE-D ships with a demo cluster makeup, it is designed to be easily adapted to other datacentres. Two data files are required: the inventory and the frequency dependence. Their filenames are specified in config.json under cluster.inventory_csv and cluster.frequency_csv and are loaded by load_cluster_inventory / load_frequency_map in src/ClusterLoader.jl.","category":"section"},{"location":"custom-cluster/#Inventory-CSV","page":"Custom cluster makeup","title":"Inventory CSV","text":"The machine inventory is a comma-separated file whose header is scanned by column name. The columns used by the loader are:\n\nHeader entry Description\ntype The name of the machine type.\nsubtype The subtype name; the full node key becomes type_subtype.\nnumber_machines The number of machines of this subtype.\ntotal_threads The number of (hyper)threads per machine.\ntotal_mem_in_Gb The amount of memory per machine in GB.\npower_min_60d The minimal power the machine draws (the idle power).\ncluster(main_Puppet_hostgroup) The cluster the machines belong to; used to filter by cluster_name.\nmodel The model of the machine (optional).\ncpu_model The CPU of the machine (optional).\ninstallation_date The installation date of the machine (optional).\n\nOther columns shipped with the demo data (representative, timestamp_check, number_decommissioned, comment, manufacturer, hepscore, total_cores, power_max_60d, ...) are ignored by the loader.\n\nFor example:\n\nrepresentative,timestamp_check,number_machines,type,subtype,comment,manufacturer,model,cpu_model,hepscore,installation_date,cluster(main_Puppet_hostgroup),total_cores,total_threads,total_mem_in_Gb,power_min_60d,power_max_60d\ndefault-DESYT3,2024-01-01T00:00:00,40,DESYT3,0,hardcoded default,,DELL PowerEdge R6525,AMD EPYC 7402 24-Core Processor,1450,2020,DEFAULT,48,96,256,112,530.1","category":"section"},{"location":"custom-cluster/#Frequency-dependence-CSV","page":"Custom cluster makeup","title":"Frequency dependence CSV","text":"The frequency dependence file maps each node key (type_subtype) to a list of measured frequency points. It is a comma-separated file with a header row (the columns hostname, type, subtype, latest measurement are ignored by the loader) followed by one row per node type. Each row lists the node type and subtype in columns 2 and 3 and, from column 5 onward, a comma-separated list of entries of the form\n\n<frequency in MHz>_<power in W>_<HEPScore>\n\none per measured frequency step, starting from the maximum (default) frequency:\n\nhostname,type,subtype,latest measurement,frequency_power_hepscore\ndefault-DESYT3,DESYT3,0,2024-01-01T00:00:00,2700_530.1_1450,2500_530.1_1450,2300_330_1102,2100_332.4_1100,1900_333.3_1102,1700_330.7_1102\n\nEach entry supplies, for the given frequency, the average power a fully occupied node draws (in W) and its HEPScore23 benchmark score. The power and HEPScore of the first (maximum) entry are used as the node's default.","category":"section"},{"location":"custom-cluster/#Adding-new-machine-types","page":"Custom cluster makeup","title":"Adding new machine types","text":"Add the machine to the inventory CSV with the required columns above.\nAdd a matching frequency dependence row using its type_subtype key.\nIf you want to change how nodes behave (rather than just their data), the model lives in src/WorkerNode.jl (WorkerNodeSpec and WorkerNode); see Development.","category":"section"},{"location":"about/#About","page":"About","title":"About","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"about/#Project-status","page":"About","title":"Project status","text":"OracleD.jl is the Julia implementation of the ORACLE-D framework, developed in parallel with the original Python version. The package version is declared in Project.toml (1.1.0); release versioning follows the parent ORACLE-D releases.","category":"section"},{"location":"about/#Copyright-and-license","page":"About","title":"Copyright and license","text":"Copyright 2023-2026 Deutsches Elektronen Synchrotron DESY and the University of Glasgow.\n\nOriginal authors: Dwayne Spiteri and Gordon Stewart.\n\nAll code in the src/ directory and subsequent subdirectory structure is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the \"License\"); you may not use this file except in compliance with the License. You may obtain a copy of the License at\n\nhttp://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\n\nUnless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software distributed under the License is distributed on an \"AS IS\" BASIS, WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied. See the License for the specific language governing permissions and limitations under the License.\n\nSee the LICENSE and NOTICE files in the repository root for details.","category":"section"},{"location":"about/#Contributors","page":"About","title":"Contributors","text":"Dwayne Spiteri, Gordon Stewart and Konrad Kockler.","category":"section"},{"location":"about/#Acknowledgements","page":"About","title":"Acknowledgements","text":"The measurements used here to categorise the different types of server come from running the HEPScore23 benchmark on compute nodes. For the server examples used in ORACLE-D these were taken by Emanuele Simili at the University of Glasgow in February 2024 and Jan Hartmann at DESY in May 2025.\n\nThe carbon intensity data for the UK is taken from the UK National Grid ESO, interpolated to fill gaps in the data, and for Germany from Agorameter and Green Grid Compass.\n\nThis code was partially written for the RF2.0 project, which has received funding from the European Union's Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement No. 101131850 and from the Swiss State Secretariat for Education Research and Innovation (SERI).","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","title":"Development","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Package-layout","page":"Development","title":"Package layout","text":"OracleD.jl/\n├── Project.toml                     # Julia project metadata (+ docs workspace)\n├── Manifest.toml                    # Resolved dependencies\n├── README.md                        # Quick-start README\n├── diagnostics_new.jl               # Whole-port performance diagnostics (developer tool)\n├── doc/\n│   └── migration-notes.md           # Python → Julia migration decisions\n├── test/\n│   └── runtests.jl                  # Unit tests (see Running the tests)\n├── docs/\n│   ├── Project.toml                 # Documentation environment\n│   ├── make.jl                      # Documenter build script\n│   └── src/                         # Documentation source (this website)\n└── src/\n    ├── OracleD.jl                   # Module + main() entry point\n    ├── Utils.jl                     # Generic utilities (paths, RNG, formatting)\n    ├── Logging.jl                   # SimLogger + run-directory creation\n    ├── Time.jl                      # SimulationTime clock\n    ├── Jobs.jl                      # Job struct\n    ├── VOJobFactory.jl              # Abstract type + 4 concrete job factories\n    ├── DataLogger.jl                # Metrics + summary output\n    ├── WorkerNode.jl                # WorkerNodeSpec + WorkerNode\n    ├── ClusterLoader.jl             # CSV inventory/frequency loading\n    ├── Cluster.jl                   # Cluster struct + per-timestep update\n    ├── JobScheduler.jl              # Initial seed + periodic job refill\n    └── Simulation.jl                # Simulation struct + main loop\n\nThe package defines a single module OracleD; everything is included via src/OracleD.jl. The module exports nothing, so names are used qualified (OracleD.Simulation, OracleD.start!, ...) unless brought into scope explicitly.","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Python-Julia-mapping","page":"Development","title":"Python → Julia mapping","text":"The source files mirror their Python counterparts (same names, same behaviour):\n\nPython Julia\nsrc/Main.py src/OracleD.jl\nsrc/simulation/Simulation.py src/Simulation.jl\nsrc/simulation/Time.py src/Time.jl\nsrc/cluster/Cluster.py src/Cluster.jl\nsrc/cluster/ClusterLoader.py src/ClusterLoader.jl\nsrc/cluster/WorkerNode.py src/WorkerNode.jl\nsrc/jobs/Jobs.py src/Jobs.jl\nsrc/jobs/VOJobFactory.py src/VOJobFactory.jl\nsrc/jobs/JobScheduler.py src/JobScheduler.jl\nsrc/datalogger/DataLogger.py src/DataLogger.jl\nsrc/util/Logging.py src/Logging.jl\n\nThe behavioural quirks of the Python code that the migration deliberately preserves (e.g. the caching of the first core-count draw, the whole-days-dropped CPU durations, and the no-op sys.exit on missing CI data) are described in doc/migration-notes.md.","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Running-the-tests","page":"Development","title":"Running the tests","text":"julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Adding-extra-options","page":"Development","title":"Adding extra options","text":"Adding new machine types is data driven: edit the cluster inventory and frequency-dependence CSV files listed in config.json (see Custom cluster makeup), no code changes required.\n\nTo alter the behaviour of nodes rather than their measurements, the model lives in src/WorkerNode.jl:\n\nWorkerNodeSpec — immutable description of one machine type (threads, memory, idle power, per-frequency powers and HEPScores, metadata).\nWorkerNode — the mutable simulated node: hosts running jobs, tracks busy cores/RAM, and applies clock-speed changes.\n\nNew job types are added by writing a new concrete struct under AbstractVOJobFactory in src/VOJobFactory.jl and specialising get_duration and require_cores! for it (multiple dispatch replaces Python's name-mangled method overriding).","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Building-the-documentation","page":"Development","title":"Building the documentation","text":"The documentation is built with Documenter.jl. The docs/ folder is listed as a workspace member of the package ([workspace] projects = [\"docs\"] in Project.toml). To build it:\n\n# from the OracleD.jl/ directory\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThis writes the generated website into docs/build/ (browse it with a local web server, e.g. python3 -m http.server --directory docs/build).","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Adding-or-editing-pages","page":"Development","title":"Adding or editing pages","text":"Markdown pages live in docs/src/. Edit existing pages or add new ones and register them in the pages vector in docs/make.jl.\nThe sidebar / page ordering is configured through the pages keyword of makedocs.\ndocs/src/api.md splices in docstrings with @docs blocks under CurrentModule = OracleD. Add a @docs entry for any new documented binding.","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Documenting-code","page":"Development","title":"Documenting code","text":"Follow the Julia conventions (see Writing Documentation): place a docstring immediately before the object it documents, start with an indented signature line, use an imperative one-liner description, and provide # Arguments / # Examples sections where helpful. Use @ref links to cross-reference other documented names and keep lines within the surrounding code's width (≈92 characters).","category":"section"},{"location":"development/#Deploying","page":"Development","title":"Deploying","text":"To publish the documentation to GitHub Pages, set the repository URL in deploydocs(...) in docs/make.jl, put the repository on a git remote, and use the standard GitHub Actions workflow for Julia documentation (e.g. julia-actions/julia-docdeploy), which calls docs/make.jl in a CI environment. Documenter pushes the generated site to the gh-pages branch. The build/ directory is generated and should not be committed.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#Configuration","page":"Configuration","title":"Configuration","text":"The simulation is configured via the config.json file at the repository root. All relevant parameters are specified there, split into sections that map onto the different parts of the code. All relative paths (data files, log directories) are resolved against the repository root.\n\nThe current running options for the savings policy are:\n\nRunning flag Description\nnone Run the nodes as standard.\ncd Clock all nodes down one frequency step below their reported maximum for the entire simulation.\ncdcd Clock all nodes down two frequency steps below their reported maximum for the entire simulation.\ncd1721 Clock all nodes down one frequency step below their reported maximum only between 5pm and 9pm.\ncdcd1721 Clock all nodes down two frequency steps below their reported maximum only between 5pm and 9pm.\nhighforecast Clock all nodes down one frequency step below their reported maximum only when the forecast carbon intensity is high (> high_CI_threshold, e.g. > 400 gCO2e/kWh).\n\nThe simulation has two encoded end conditions:\n\nall the jobs sent to the cluster have been completed; or\nthe amount of time in seconds specified by Simulation.simulation_length has passed.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#Simulation","page":"Configuration","title":"Simulation","text":"Variable Description\ndesired_starttime The time at which the simulation starts (\"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\"). Leaving it as nothing defaults to the current clock time.\nsimulation_length The maximum duration the simulation may run for, in seconds.\ntimestep The timestep between updates, in seconds.\nsavings_policy The savings policy (see the table above).\nrng_seed (Julia addition.) A seed for Random.seed!, making the run reproducible.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#carbon_intensity","page":"Configuration","title":"carbon_intensity","text":"Variable Description\nfolder The folder where the carbon intensity data is stored.\nfilename The filename of the carbon intensity data file.\nhigh_CI_threshold The threshold (gCO2e/kWh) above which carbon intensity is considered \"high\".","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#jobs","page":"Configuration","title":"jobs","text":"Variable Description\ninitial_mix The mix of jobs submitted to the cluster at the start. A dictionary mapping job type → number of jobs. Implemented job types are \"ATLAS\", \"LHCb\" and \"GridPP\"; any other name produces a basic VO job.\nregular_incoming_mix A mix of jobs submitted at regular intervals (same format as initial_mix). If empty, no jobs are refilled.\nincoming_timestep The timestep (seconds) between successive job submissions.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#output","page":"Configuration","title":"output","text":"Variable Description\nverbosity INFO-level logging detail. Valid values are \"low\", \"medium\" and \"high\".\ndebug If true, DEBUG messages are recorded in addition to warnings and errors.\nlog_dir Optional. Directory where per-run log folders are written (default logs/runs).\nrun_label Optional. Human-readable label added to the run folder name. If omitted, the label is generated from the number of initial jobs and the savings policy.\n\nEach run creates a folder named YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS_<run-label> under log_dir containing simulation.log, summary.txt, summary.json, parameters.txt and a copy of the run config.json. summary.json contains both the simulation parameters and the final summary metrics, for machine-readable comparison between runs.\n\nVerbosity behaviour:\n\nlow: only high-level lifecycle messages (for example simulation creation).\nmedium: major progress messages (for example loading data and the end-condition messages).\nhigh: the most detailed INFO logs, including per-job start/finish entries from the data logger.\n\nIf output.verbosity is not one of the three valid values, ORACLE-D logs a warning and defaults to high.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#cluster","page":"Configuration","title":"cluster","text":"Variable Description\ncluster_name The name of the cluster; used to filter the inventory on the cluster(main_Puppet_hostgroup) column.\ninventory_csv The CSV file with the cluster inventory.\nfrequency_csv The CSV file with the frequency dependence of the cluster.\nstrict Whether the program terminates on incomplete frequency-dependence data entries (true) or logs a warning and continues (false).\n\nSee Custom cluster makeup for the exact CSV formats.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration/#Example","page":"Configuration","title":"Example","text":"A minimal configuration that runs 100,000 GridPP jobs on the default DESY cluster, starting 2024-01-16 16:00, for up to one week, with highforecast running conditions:\n\n{\n    \"Simulation\": {\n        \"desired_starttime\": \"2024-01-16 16:00\",\n        \"simulation_length\": 604800,\n        \"timestep\": 600,\n        \"savings_policy\": \"highforecast\",\n        \"rng_seed\": 42\n    },\n    \"carbon_intensity\": {\n        \"folder\": \"data/carbon_intensity/\",\n        \"filename\": \"de_carbon_Intensity_2024_15min.csv\",\n        \"high_CI_threshold\": 400\n    },\n    \"cluster\": {\n        \"cluster_name\": \"DEFAULT\",\n        \"inventory_csv\": \"data/cluster/default-machinegroups_inventory.csv\",\n        \"frequency_csv\": \"data/cluster/default-frequency_dependence.csv\",\n        \"strict\": false\n    },\n    \"jobs\": {\n        \"initial_mix\": {\"GridPP\": 100000},\n        \"regular_incoming_mix\": {\"GridPP\": 1000},\n        \"incoming_timestep\": 3600\n    },\n    \"output\": {\n        \"verbosity\": \"medium\",\n        \"debug\": false,\n        \"log_dir\": \"logs/runs\",\n        \"run_label\": \"MyFirstRun\"\n    }\n}","category":"section"},{"location":"#OracleD.jl","page":"Home","title":"OracleD.jl","text":"Optimised Resource Analysis and Carbon Legacy Estimator for Data centres.\n\nOracleD.jl is the Julia implementation of the ORACLE-D framework: a modular simulator of Grid-computing sites. It models a cluster of compute nodes processing HEP-style job workloads and estimates, time-step by time-step, the energy consumed and the carbon emitted while doing so — including the effect of running the site under different energy-saving policies.\n\nThe package is a direct migration of the original [Python implementation] (https://github.com/desy/ORACLE-D). It reads the same config.json and data files from the repository root and produces the same console output and per-run artefacts (simulation.log, summary.txt, summary.json, parameters.txt and a copy of the run config.json under logs/runs/). The Python-original behavioural quirks that the migration deliberately preserves are catalogued in doc/migration-notes.md.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Table-of-contents","page":"Home","title":"Table of contents","text":"Pages = [\"index.md\", \"configuration.md\", \"custom-cluster.md\", \"development.md\", \"api.md\", \"about.md\"]\nDepth = 2","category":"section"},{"location":"#Features","page":"Home","title":"Features","text":"Modular components. The computing site (WorkerNode), the work submitted to it (VOJobFactory, JobScheduler) and the running mode (savings_policy) are all easy to swap or extend.\nEnergy & carbon accounting. Every worker node reports its energy use per time-step (10 minutes by default); this is multiplied by the grid carbon intensity to estimate emissions, with total and peak-time (5pm–9pm) breakdowns.\nEnergy-saving policies. cd, cdcd, cd1721, cdcd1721 and highforecast clock the nodes down (and back up) either permanently, at fixed times of day, or according to forecast carbon intensity.\nJust-in-case reproducibility. An optional rng_seed in the configuration makes a run reproducible.\nPer-run artefacts. Every run produces a summary \n\n(summary.txt, summary.json, parameters.txt, simulation.log)","category":"section"},{"location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","title":"Quick start","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#Requirements","page":"Home","title":"Requirements","text":"Julia 1.12 or newer (the migration was done with the production release 1.12.6, e.g. via juliaup).","category":"section"},{"location":"#Instantiate-the-environment","page":"Home","title":"Instantiate the environment","text":"From the OracleD.jl/ package directory, resolve the dependencies once:\n\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'","category":"section"},{"location":"#Run-the-baseline-simulation","page":"Home","title":"Run the baseline simulation","text":"julia --project src/OracleD.jl\n\nThis runs the job mix configured in the repository-root config.json — by default 40,000 ATLAS and 10,000 LHCb jobs on the default DESY Grid compute cluster starting 2024-01-16 16:00, without special running conditions and at medium verbosity. The output is written to a new timestamped folder under logs/runs/. All relative paths in the configuration are resolved against the repository root, so the command works from any working directory.\n\nAlternatively, from the Julia REPL:\n\nusing OracleD\nOracleD.main()      # OracleD exports nothing; qualify names explicitly","category":"section"},{"location":"#Run-the-tests","page":"Home","title":"Run the tests","text":"julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","category":"section"},{"location":"#Build-this-documentation","page":"Home","title":"Build this documentation","text":"julia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nSee Building the documentation for details.","category":"section"}]
}
